you are watching a clip from the john  perry channel  genetics and evolution  information theory has been used a lot  in in biology shannon as you know worked  for the bell telephone company and so he  was concerned with the economics of  information transfer so he wanted to  think of think of ways in which  information could be most economically  encoded and he came with the idea of the  bit of information as the fundamental  unit of information information for him  meant reduction of  uncertainty it was actually measured  as reduction of uncertainty and his  formula  measured it like that one of the things  that comes out of  shannon type mathematics is this idea  that  information as a commodity  is easily  translated into other media  so it doesn't matter  whether it's in the form of punch paper  tape or  little lips on um magnetic tape or a  little  um  noise morse code or little little noises  going down a wire  if if it's  in the same if it's translated from one  digital code to another then it can be  faithfully rendered at the other end  with a certain  possibility of  error  and  i very much like the idea i've been very  inspired by the idea of information as a  commodity which as you just described it  can be translated from one medium to  another and retains its value  retains its meaning  throughout all these different  translations  sharon was concerned with the  economics of information transfer such  that nothing is lost or little as  possible is lost that's different from  the semantics from actually what what  the information means which is a whole  separate subject  let's try and unpack this even more so  what is information in general  in your view and then what is  information  in genetics specifically  maybe maybe that could be helpful  well as i said shannon described it as  or measured it as reduction in  uncertainty so when it's when it's  transferred from one  place to another in in human terms  you you start with the idea that there's  a sender and a receiver  and  and the receiver doesn't know something  that the sender does know  so um shannon was  was aware that something like the sun  rose this morning contains much less  information than the sun did not rise  this morning because the receiver  already expects that the sun will rise  and therefore i mean the the reduction  of of uncertainty the surprise value  is is is less so um the one bit of  information is is  that which halves your uncertainty  um if you have something like um  uh if if if i turn up a card for an  ordinary pack of cards and i ask you to  guess  um the  um  the cut the color of the card your  uncertainty is is exactly  um  twofold it could be red or it could be  black so if i tell you it's black that's  one bit of information it's it's been  reduced by one  by what's been divided in in half  um if i tell you it's a  it's a heart  um that's two bits of information  because you don't know whether first  whether it's a color and secondly given  that given that it's red say whether it  could be a heart or a diamond so that's  how he measured information and you can  measure information exactly in the same  way  in the genetic code it being a  quaternary code rather than a binary  code it's a little bit more complicated  i have had on my show dr david fialo and  we were talking about you know what is  information in biology and he was  explaining that the base information in  biology is nucleotide sequences sequence  information is the thing that's being  communicated from parent to child  but that doesn't necessarily have any  sort of meaning to it right it could be  a random chain of of rna for example  claude shannon wasn't interested in  meaning he was just interested in how do  you transfer information from one thing  to another if you talk about this in  biological terms he's interested in  replication how do you get a system  going that can replicate a chain of rna  for example but in biology we also care  about meaning and what i mean by that is  that you know the the  specific sequences in our dna help us  better survive and reproduce  and  what you've so beautifully spent your  career explaining is really i guess you  could say is where that meaning comes  from this is what darwin explained  through the process of natural selection  as soon as you have a system capable of  replicating you can get darwinian  evolution  and just automatically  those sequences which happen to be  better at reproducing than others  end up becoming more common  in the population  and you get this explosion of  not just information but useful  information information that's useful  in the project of surviving and  reproducing it is very amazing and it  completely gives the lie to anybody to  the things you have to have a creator  uh to start these things off it really  does show quite an increase in  complexity increase in adaptive  efficiency  as the generations  go by  so that was just a clip for my  conversation with richard dawkins you  can see the whole thing there's a link  to that down below subscribe to this  channel if you enjoyed it  i upload clips pretty regularly talks  and conversations and presentations that  i've done on genetics and evolution and  i am gearing up to start doing weekly  videos again here that are completely  new completely fresh
 

